for jonathan chan the pictures in the  tenement museum bring back vivid  memories in 1989 he was a member of the  Student Union and witnessed the june  fourth massacre on tanaman square chen  says he owes it to his friends from back  then to not let the events be forgotten  when they realized reaffirm the Hong  Kong I was saying to say always set the  same stuff code how to the work go tell  the people on come on songs so I had to  talk about this then then the  opportunity I wise to keep the memories  to our life yes activists from Hong Kong  created this museum as a place for  remembrance they managed to complete the  project in spite of difficulties all  forms of commemoration are forbidden in  mainland China that's why the museum  caters especially to visitors from there  for Chinese from the mainland a visit  here is taboo no one wants to speak in  front of a camera especially younger  generation will say oh it's a shock to  me that I don't know this happened for  some of the older generation or this we  have not really seen for already 25  years and it refresh our memory and we  hope that they will go back to China to  spread what they have seen in Hong Kong  inside China so that people would know  the historical truth and will want to  you know demand for justice  jonathan chan also wants justice he  believes the death of his comrades  changed china forever chan says the  massacre revealed the communist regimes  true face and drove a wedge between the  people and the government but he refuses  to give up hope that the dream people  fought for 25 years ago will one day  come true
 

